FAQs
1. What does TPH stand for in PPF?
TPH stands for thermoplastic hybrid, a blended-polymer film used as a budget alternative to TPU. Some sellers describe it as thermoplastic polyolefin, so confirm the exact material with your installer.
2. Is TPU the better option over TPH?
Yes, for most owners. TPU is more flexible, resists impacts better, stays clearer, and usually self-heals light scratches. TPH costs less but offers basic protection and a shorter life.
3. Does TPH PPF have any self-healing?
Usually not. Self-healing is found in premium TPU films, which recover light scratches with heat. TPH films typically offer limited self-healing or none.
4. For how long does TPU PPF last?
Quality TPU film can last up to 10 years with proper installation and care. Climate, driving conditions, and maintenance affect its life, so ask your installer about the warranty.
5. Is ceramic coating possible over TPU PPF?
Yes. A PPF-safe ceramic coating adds a hydrophobic layer, making TPU film easier to clean and more resistant to stains. Check with your installer for compatible products.
